=== task: mcq medium
title: Where is the largest?
prompt: A min-heap holds many different values. Where can its largest value be?
* Only in a leaf: an item with no children :: Right. Any item with a child is smaller than that child, so it can't be the largest.
- Always at the end of the list :: The last item is a leaf, but the largest can be any leaf. In `[1, 9, 2, 10, 11, 3, 4]` it's 11, at index 4.
- At index 1 or 2 :: Those places hold the candidates for the second smallest, not the largest.
- Anywhere at all :: An item with a child can't be the largest, because its child is bigger.
hint: What does the rule say about an item that has a child?
hint: Which items have no children?
right: In a leaf: every item with a child is smaller than that child. The leaves fill the second half of the list, so finding the largest still means checking about n/2 items.
wrong: Every parent is smaller than its children, so the largest value has no children: it's one of the leaves, which fill the second half of the list.

