=== task: multi hard
title: Heap or search tree?
prompt: Choose **every** statement that is true when comparing a min-heap with a balanced binary search tree holding the same n values.
* The heap finds the smallest value in O(1), the tree in O(log n) :: Right. The heap keeps it at index 0; the tree has to walk its left links.
* Both add a value in O(log n) :: Right. The heap sifts up one path; the tree walks down one path.
* The heap needs no node objects or links :: Right. Positions in the list do the job of links.
* The heap stays balanced, however many values are pushed :: Right. It's always a complete tree.
- The heap can check whether any value is in it in O(log n) :: The rule doesn't say which branch holds a value, so searching a heap is O(n).
- The heap can list all its values in sorted order in O(n) :: Only by popping them one by one: O(n log n). The tree does it in O(n) with inorder.
hint: Which structure keeps the smallest value in a fixed place?
hint: Which one tells you whether to go left or right when looking for a value?
right: A heap wins on the smallest value and stays balanced with no links; a search tree wins at finding any value and at listing everything in order.
wrong: The heap is simpler and keeps the minimum at index 0, always balanced. But it can't search for a value or list everything in order quickly: that's what a search tree is for.

